 "segments": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 1.88,
   "text": "It's a freezing night in northern Britain."
  },
  {
   "start": 2.38,
   "end": 6.32,
   "text": "You're nineteen, you're a long way from home, and you're standing on top of a wall."
  },
  {
   "start": 6.84,
   "end": 7.84,
   "text": "Fog rolls in from the north."
  },
  {
   "start": 8.38,
   "end": 10.18,
   "text": "Somewhere out there, something is moving."
  },
  {
   "start": 10.52,
   "end": 11.5,
   "text": "You grip your spear."
  },
  {
   "start": 12.02,
   "end": 13.32,
   "text": "You squint into the dark."
  },
  {
   "start": 13.52,
   "end": 14.98,
   "text": "A shape climbs out of the mist."
  },
  {
   "start": 15.18,
   "end": 18.4,
   "text": "It's a goat. Behind the goat, a man walks right up to the wall."
  },
  {
   "start": 18.7,
   "end": 19.54,
   "text": "He doesn't climb it."
  },
  {
   "start": 19.72,
   "end": 20.6,
   "text": "He doesn't attack it."
  },
  {
   "start": 21.04,
   "end": 24.32,
   "text": "He knocks. Because this wall, built by the greatest army on Earth,"
  },
  {
   "start": 24.5,
   "end": 24.96,
   "text": "has a door."
  },
  {
   "start": 25.26,
   "end": 26.02,
   "text": "Actually,"
  },
  {
   "start": 26.2,
   "end": 27.78,
   "text": "it has about eighty of them."
  },
  {
   "start": 28.0,
   "end": 33.42,
   "text": "Let's go back. It's the year 122. The Roman Empire is close to the biggest it will ever be."
  },
  {
   "start": 33.74,
   "end": 37.46,
   "text": "It stretches from the deserts of Syria to the drizzle of northern Britain."
  },
  {
   "start": 37.96,
   "end": 40.58,
   "text": "Britain has been a Roman province for almost eighty years."
  },
  {
   "start": 41.18,
   "end": 44.42,
   "text": "Historians think around sixty million people live under one emperor."
  },
  {
   "start": 44.72,
   "end": 48.88,
   "text": "And at the far northwest corner of all that power, Rome does something strange."
  },
  {
   "start": 49.06,
   "end": 52.0,
   "text": "It starts building a wall. Seventy-three miles of it,"
  },
  {
   "start": 52.32,
   "end": 52.92,
   "text": "coast to coast,"
  },
  {
   "start": 53.1,
   "end": 55.74,
   "text": "from the River Tyne in the east to the Solway Firth in the west."
  },
  {
   "start": 56.0,
   "end": 57.16,
   "text": "Now think about that."
  },
  {
   "start": 57.46,
   "end": 60.7,
   "text": "This is the empire that conquered Gaul, Egypt, and Greece."
  },
  {
   "start": 60.88,
   "end": 61.42,
   "text": "It doesn't hide."
  },
  {
   "start": 61.78,
   "end": 62.64,
   "text": "It invades."
  },
  {
   "start": 62.98,
   "end": 67.62,
   "text": "So why would the most powerful army on the planet spend years stacking rocks at the end of the world?"
  },
  {
   "start": 68.0,
   "end": 69.24,
   "text": "Was Rome scared of someone?"
  },
  {
   "start": 69.42,
   "end": 73.1,
   "text": "Who lived on the other side? And if the wall was meant to keep people out,"
  },
  {
   "start": 73.32,
   "end": 75.48,
   "text": "why did it have a gate every single mile?"
  },
  {
   "start": 76.2,
   "end": 76.92,
   "text": "That's the question."
  },
  {
   "start": 77.18,
   "end": 79.08,
   "text": "And the answer isn't what most people think."
  },
  {
   "start": 79.44,
   "end": 82.0,
   "text": "To get there, we need to meet the man whose name is on it."
  },
  {
   "start": 82.2,
   "end": 84.0,
   "text": "Publius Aelius Hadrianus. Hadrian,"
  },
  {
   "start": 84.62,
   "end": 84.92,
   "text": "for short."
  },
  {
   "start": 85.14,
   "end": 90.08,
   "text": "He became emperor in 117, and he looked different from the emperors before him. He had a beard."
  },
  {
   "start": 90.58,
   "end": 91.1,
   "text": "A real,"
  },
  {
   "start": 91.34,
   "end": 92.36,
   "text": "full beard. Before him,"
  },
  {
   "start": 92.64,
   "end": 95.62,
   "text": "Roman emperors were almost always clean-shaven."
  },
  {
   "start": 96.16,
   "end": 96.74,
   "text": "After him,"
  },
  {
   "start": 97.28,
   "end": 101.74,
   "text": "almost every emperor for a century grew one. But the beard wasn't the biggest change."
  },
  {
   "start": 101.98,
   "end": 105.96,
   "text": "His predecessor, Trajan, had pushed the empire all the way into Mesopotamia."
  },
  {
   "start": 106.22,
   "end": 108.92,
   "text": "Hadrian looked at the map and gave those new provinces back."
  },
  {
   "start": 109.28,
   "end": 112.36,
   "text": "He wanted an empire that stopped growing and started holding."
  },
  {
   "start": 112.6,
   "end": 114.64,
   "text": "In his mind, the borders were finally done."
  },
  {
   "start": 114.88,
   "end": 117.32,
   "text": "And Britain was the messiest border of all."
  },
  {
   "start": 117.5,
   "end": 122.58,
   "text": "Forty years earlier, a Roman general named Agricola had marched deep into what is now Scotland."
  },
  {
   "start": 123.22,
   "end": 125.06,
   "text": "In the year 83 or 84,"
  },
  {
   "start": 125.28,
   "end": 132.08,
   "text": "he crushed the Caledonians at a battle called Mons Graupius. Rome could have taken the whole island. Instead,"
  },
  {
   "start": 132.3,
   "end": 133.36,
   "text": "it pulled back."
  },
  {
   "start": 133.74,
   "end": 136.52,
   "text": "The army was needed elsewhere, and the Highlands were cold,"
  },
  {
   "start": 137.16,
   "end": 138.16,
   "text": "wet, and hard to tax."
  },
  {
   "start": 138.6,
   "end": 143.66,
   "text": "The frontier slid south, to a line of forts along a road called the Stanegate. And when Hadrian took power,"
  },
  {
   "start": 144.02,
   "end": 150.72,
   "text": "his own biography says the Britons could not be kept under Roman control. So in 122, Hadrian did something emperors rarely did."
  },
  {
   "start": 151.04,
   "end": 152.42,
   "text": "He came to see it himself."
  },
  {
   "start": 152.76,
   "end": 153.68,
   "text": "He crossed the sea,"
  },
  {
   "start": 153.92,
   "end": 156.76,
   "text": "rode north, and walked the land between the Tyne and the Solway,"
  },
  {
   "start": 156.94,
   "end": 159.4,
   "text": "one of the narrowest necks of the whole island."
  },
  {
   "start": 159.68,
   "end": 163.44,
   "text": "Out there, beyond the hills, were tribes Rome had never fully conquered."
  },
  {
   "start": 164.28,
   "end": 166.16,
   "text": "To the south were Britons Rome had conquered,"
  },
  {
   "start": 166.46,
   "end": 167.72,
   "text": "but didn't completely trust."
  },
  {
   "start": 167.9,
   "end": 169.88,
   "text": "Hadrian was a serious amateur architect,"
  },
  {
   "start": 170.16,
   "end": 175.22,
   "text": "and many historians think the basic plan came from him. He stood in the middle and made a decision."
  },
  {
   "start": 175.44,
   "end": 177.76,
   "text": "He didn't order a war. He ordered a wall."
  },
  {
   "start": 177.96,
   "end": 182.08,
   "text": "And the soldiers who had to build it were about to have a very long six years."
  }
 ],
